body:
The view storage uses an in-memory Map which means all saved views will be lost when the server restarts. This violates the expectation of "saved views" being persistent.

While the PR description mentions this is "DB-agnostic interface" and uses in-memory storage temporarily, this should be clearly documented in the code. Consider adding a comment warning about the temporary nature and the need for future persistence implementation, or at minimum log a warning on startup indicating views are not persisted.
```suggestion
    private engine: IDataEngine;
    /**
     * Temporary in-memory storage for SavedView definitions.
     *
     * IMPORTANT:
     * - This implementation is NON-PERSISTENT: all views will be lost when the
     *   process restarts.
     * - It exists only as a DB-agnostic placeholder for the protocol surface.
     * - Replace with a durable backing store (e.g. SQL/NoSQL) when wiring this
     *   protocol into a production environment.
     */
    private viewStorage: Map<string, SavedView> = new Map();

    constructor(engine: IDataEngine) {
        this.engine = engine;
        // Warn at runtime so operators understand that saved views are not durable.
        // This avoids surprising data loss when the server process is restarted.
        console.warn(
            '[ObjectStackProtocol] Saved views are stored in-memory only and will be lost on restart. ' +
            'Configure a persistent backing implementation before using this in production.'
        );
```

The try-catch block that only rethrows the error serves no purpose and adds unnecessary code complexity. Either remove the try-catch entirely or add meaningful error handling/transformation.

If the intent was to catch and convert errors to a standardized format, the catch block should return a MetadataCacheResponse with an error field instead of rethrowing.
```suggestion
        const item = SchemaRegistry.getItem(type, name);
        if (!item) {
            throw new Error(`Metadata item ${type}/${name} not found`);
        }

        // Calculate ETag (simple hash of the stringified metadata)
        const content = JSON.stringify(item);
        const hash = simpleHash(content);
        const etag = { value: hash, weak: false };

        // Check If-None-Match header
        if (cacheRequest?.ifNoneMatch) {
            const clientEtag = cacheRequest.ifNoneMatch.replace(/^"(.*)"$/, '$1').replace(/^W\/"(.*)"$/, '$1');
            if (clientEtag === hash) {
                // Return 304 Not Modified
                return {
                    notModified: true,
                    etag,
                };
            }
        }

        // Return full metadata with cache headers
        return {
            data: item,
            etag,
            lastModified: new Date().toISOString(),
            cacheControl: {
                directives: ['public', 'max-age'],
                maxAge: 3600, // 1 hour
            },
            notModified: false,
        };
```

The Cache-Control header construction is incorrect. When maxAge is present, it should be included as part of the max-age directive value, not concatenated as a separate directive.

The current code produces: "public, max-age, max-age=3600" which is invalid.
It should produce: "public, max-age=3600"

Fix by changing the logic to only add 'max-age' to the directives array if there's no maxAge value, or construct the header string more carefully.
```suggestion
                            const cacheDirectives = [...result.cacheControl.directives];
                            if (result.cacheControl.maxAge !== undefined) {
                                // Ensure we don't end up with both "max-age" and "max-age=<value>"
                                const filteredDirectives = cacheDirectives.filter(
                                    (d) => d.toLowerCase() !== 'max-age',
                                );
                                filteredDirectives.push(`max-age=${result.cacheControl.maxAge}`);
                                res.header('Cache-Control', filteredDirectives.join(', '));
